---
import Layout, { REPO } from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { bookmarkletUrl } from "../../../packages/browser/bookmarklet-url.ts";
import bundle from "../../../packages/browser/bookmarklet.js?raw";
const href = bookmarkletUrl(bundle);
---

<Layout
  title="Install the deadhead CLI, ESLint plugin and bookmarklet"
  description="Install and run deadhead: the CLI, the bookmarklet, the ESLint plugin, suppression comments and CI configuration."
>
    <h1>Install</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Node 24.8 or newer; both packages are ESM only. No configuration is
      required to start. The defaults are the point.
    </p>

    <h2>CLI</h2>
    <pre><code>npx deadhead dist
npm install --save-dev deadhead</code></pre>
    <p>
      It takes files and directories, and expands globs itself so they
      behave the same in every shell:
    </p>
    <pre><code>deadhead dist                     # walk a directory for .html and .htm
deadhead "src/**/*.html"          # quote it; the CLI does the expanding
deadhead --format=sarif dist      # stylish (default), json, sarif
deadhead --fail-on=harmful dist   # exit 1 only on harmful findings
deadhead --fix dist               # rewrite files, then report what is left
deadhead --jobs 4 dist            # large trees in worker threads
deadhead --quiet dist             # only findings at or above --fail-on
deadhead --output-file r.sarif --format=sarif dist
deadhead --list-rules             # every rule, then exit
cat page.html | deadhead - --stdin-filename page.html</code></pre>
    <p>
      Exit codes are the CI contract: <strong>0</strong> nothing at or above the{" "}
      <code>--fail-on</code> threshold, <strong>1</strong> threshold met,{" "}
      <strong>2</strong> usage, config or I/O error. A broken invocation never
      looks like a clean run.
    </p>

    <h2>Pages and fragments</h2>
    <p>
      A file with a doctype or an <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> tag is a page.
      Anything else is a fragment: a layout partial, a component, a template.
      A fragment is checked for the markup it holds, never for head content it
      lacks, since other files supply that. Markup outside a written{" "}
      <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> gets both the head
      and the body rules.
    </p>

    <h2>Fixes are text edits, never re-serialised markup</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--fix</code> splices byte ranges out of the original file. It never
      parses your document and prints it back, because that rewrites quote
      style and attribute order across the
      whole file. Whitespace and character references shift with them, turning
      a one-line fix into a thousand-line diff.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things are never fixed automatically: a rule declaring{" "}
      <code>fix: &lbrace; op: "none" &rbrace;</code>, and any rule whose
      detectability is{" "}
      <code>partial</code>. If the rule is not certain, it does not get to edit
      your file.
    </p>

    <h2>Suppressing a finding</h2>
    <pre><code>&lt;!-- deadhead-disable-next-line meta/http-equiv-x-ua-compatible --&gt;
&lt;!-- deadhead-disable link/shortcut-icon --&gt;
&lt;!-- deadhead-enable --&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      A bare <code>deadhead-disable</code> with no rule id suppresses everything
      until the matching enable. The engine reads the contents of{" "}
      <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;code&gt;</code>,{" "}
      <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;samp&gt;</code> and{" "}
      <code>&lt;kbd&gt;</code> as content rather than markup, and skips{" "}
      <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;noembed&gt;</code>,{" "}
      <code>&lt;noframes&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;noscript&gt;</code>,{" "}
      <code>&lt;plaintext&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> and{" "}
      <code>&lt;xmp&gt;</code>, which an HTML parser reads as text.
    </p>

    <h2>Bookmarklet</h2>
    <p>Drag this link to your bookmarks bar, then click it on any page:</p>
    <p><a href={href}>deadhead</a></p>
    <p>
      It reads the rendered page, where frameworks have already assembled the head,
      and draws its findings in a panel. It loads nothing and runs no eval, so a
      strict Content-Security-Policy does not block it. Its full report button
      re-reads the page's HTML as the server sent it and opens it on{" "}
      <a href="/try">Try</a> for line numbers, advice and the fixed HTML. The HTML
      travels in the link fragment, which no server receives; where the page's CSP
      blocks the re-read, the button sends the rendered DOM. To run it from DevTools
      instead, paste <a href="/bookmarklet.js">bookmarklet.js</a> into Sources → Snippets.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/deadhead.css">deadhead.css</a> does the same without script: add it to
      a page and the offending tags outline themselves.
    </p>

    <h2>ESLint</h2>
    <p>
      <code>eslint-plugin-deadhead</code> reports the same findings through{" "}
      <code>@html-eslint/parser</code>, with autofix. It is a convenience for
      editor feedback: if your head tags live in JSX, Vue or Svelte, an HTML
      parser will not see them, and the bookmarklet will. It needs ESLint 10.
    </p>
    <pre><code>npm install --save-dev eslint eslint-plugin-deadhead</code></pre>
    <pre><code>// eslint.config.js
import &lbrace; defineConfig &rbrace; from "eslint/config";
import deadhead from "eslint-plugin-deadhead";

export default defineConfig([deadhead.configs.recommended]);</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>recommended</code> turns on every harmful and deprecated rule:
      harmful ones are errors and the rest warnings. <code>all</code> adds the
      unnecessary ones. Rule names are the rule ids:{" "}
      <code>deadhead/meta/keywords</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Full flag reference, configuration file and baseline documentation live in
      the{" "}
      <a href={REPO}>README</a>.
    </p>
</Layout>
